<BaseLayout title="Part VII · Response and Lattice Dynamics" description="How declared perturbations produce response derivatives, force constants, phonon modes, and electron–phonon coupling objects without conflating methods, wavevectors, or downstream observables." current="core">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/core/')}>Core</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Part VII</span>
  </nav>

  <p class="secondary">Part VII</p>
  <h1>Response and Lattice Dynamics</h1>
  <p class="lede">A ground state describes one system under one set of external conditions. Response begins when we declare a perturbation and ask how that state changes. For nuclear displacements, the relevant derivatives become interatomic force constants; periodicity and mass then organize them into phonons. The same displacement-induced electronic potential finally provides the object that couples electronic states to lattice vibrations.</p>

  <h2>A ground state becomes a response problem only after a perturbation is named</h2>
  <p>Part VI treated energy, force, and curvature as information on a Born–Oppenheimer surface. Curvature already hints at response: move one nucleus slightly, and forces elsewhere change. But “the response of the material” is incomplete language. We must say what is changed, which quantity is observed, whether the change is static or time-dependent, and which mechanical and electrical boundary conditions are held fixed.</p>
  <p>Let a small parameter <math class="math-inline" aria-label="lambda"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> control a declared perturbation and let <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denote the quantity whose change we want. To first order around the unperturbed state, response is the slope that converts the small cause into the small effect:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The first-order change in A equals the response coefficient chi times the perturbation, where chi is the derivative of A with respect to the perturbation at zero perturbation">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.75em">
          <mtr><mtd><mi>δ</mi><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta A=\chi_&#123;A\lambda&#125;\delta\lambda,\qquad \chi_&#123;A\lambda&#125;=\left.\frac&#123;\partial A&#125;&#123;\partial\lambda&#125;\right|_&#123;\lambda=0&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the first line from right to left. A specified perturbation of size <math class="math-inline" aria-label="delta lambda"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is applied; the response coefficient <math class="math-inline" aria-label="chi A lambda"><semantics><msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_&#123;A\lambda&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> tells how strongly the chosen quantity reacts; their product gives only the first-order change <math class="math-inline" aria-label="delta A"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta A</annotation></semantics></math>. A large perturbation can require nonlinear terms, so this equation is a local statement rather than a universal proportionality.</p>
  <p>The compact symbol <math class="math-inline" aria-label="chi"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> can represent a number, a tensor, or a function of two positions. In a periodic system it may also depend on perturbation wavevector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and frequency <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Those labels are not decoration: a uniform static electric field, a short-wavelength displacement, and an oscillating field ask different physical questions and therefore have different response functions.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Ch. 21; Baroni et al., <cite>Reviews of Modern Physics</cite> 73, 515 (2001), Sec. II.C.</p>

  <figure class="response-map" aria-labelledby="response-map-caption">
    <ol class="atlas-panel-grid atlas-panel-grid--3">
      <li><strong>Declare the cause</strong><span>Displacement, strain, electric field, or another specified perturbation, with its wavevector, frequency, and boundary conditions.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Let the state adjust</strong><span>The density, potential, orbitals, and occupations respond consistently within the chosen theory.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Read the derivative</strong><span>Force constant, dielectric response, susceptibility, or another observable-specific coefficient.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption id="response-map-caption">A response coefficient belongs to a declared cause–effect pair. Naming only the calculated quantity is not enough to define the response problem.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Finite perturbations and linear response approach the same derivative</h2>
  <p>There are two natural ways to obtain a derivative. The first evaluates nearby, fully self-consistent ground states: displace a nucleus by a small positive and negative amount, calculate the new forces, and form a finite difference. For a periodic crystal, a real-space displacement pattern may require a supercell large enough to contain the force response.</p>
  <p>The second differentiates the self-consistent Kohn–Sham problem at the reference state. A small external change produces a first-order change in the Kohn–Sham potential, orbitals, and density; the induced density in turn changes the Hartree and exchange–correlation parts of the potential. Solving this coupled first-order problem is the central idea of density-functional perturbation theory, or DFPT.</p>
  <p>Differentiating the Kohn–Sham eigenproblem produces a linear Sternheimer equation. The known reference-state Kohn–Sham operator acts on the unknown first-order change of an occupied orbital; the driving term is the first-order self-consistent potential acting on the unperturbed orbital. For a fixed-occupation gapped reference, components inside the occupied subspace are fixed by the orthonormality convention, while the physically relevant density response comes from changing that subspace toward unoccupied directions. Metals add a response of the occupations and Fermi surface. The resulting orbital and occupation changes build an induced density, that density rebuilds the first-order potential, and the coupled linear response is solved self-consistently. This route obtains the needed response without explicitly summing over every empty eigenstate.</p>

  <figure class="response-routes" aria-labelledby="response-routes-caption">
    <div class="atlas-panel-grid">
      <section>
        <h3>Finite displacement</h3>
        <p>Solve nearby self-consistent ground states, compare their forces, and take a controlled numerical derivative.</p>
      </section>
      <p class="same-response"><strong>Same target</strong><span>Derivative of the same declared Born–Oppenheimer surface</span></p>
      <section>
        <h3>DFPT</h3>
        <p>Linearize the self-consistent equations and solve directly for the first-order electronic response at the reference state.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="response-routes-caption">Finite displacement and DFPT are different computational routes to the same harmonic derivative when the structure, Hamiltonian, boundary conditions, and approximations match. They are not different physical theories of phonons.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The two routes have different numerical error sources. Finite differences must balance displacement size against numerical noise and control supercell interactions. DFPT must converge the self-consistent first-order solution and the reciprocal-space representation of the perturbation. Agreement can be a valuable cross-method check only after these independent controls and the underlying physical model have been aligned.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Baroni et al., Secs. II.C and IV.B. The review derives self-consistent linear response and contrasts it with frozen-phonon calculations.</p>

  <h2 id="phonons-and-lattice-dynamics">Nuclear response turns curvature into interatomic force constants</h2>
  <p>Now choose nuclear displacement as the perturbation. Let atom <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in a reference cell move along Cartesian direction <math class="math-inline" aria-label="alpha"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and atom <math class="math-inline" aria-label="B"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in the cell displaced by lattice vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> move along direction <math class="math-inline" aria-label="beta"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Their interatomic force constant is:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The interatomic force constant is the second derivative of the Born Oppenheimer energy with respect to two atomic displacements, equivalently minus the derivative of one atomic force with respect to the other displacement">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac>
          <mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_&#123;A\alpha,B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=\frac&#123;\partial^2U&#125;&#123;\partial u_&#123;A\alpha&#125;(0)\,\partial u_&#123;B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)&#125;=-\frac&#123;\partial F_&#123;A\alpha&#125;(0)&#125;&#123;\partial u_&#123;B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This equation reads one physical object in two ways. As an energy curvature, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Phi"><semantics><mi>Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> measures how two displacement coordinates are coupled. As a force response, it asks how moving atom <math class="math-inline" aria-label="B"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> changes the force on atom <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. The minus sign comes from <math class="math-inline" aria-label="force equals minus energy gradient"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo>∇</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=-\nabla U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  <p>The reference structure must be sufficiently stationary for an ordinary harmonic-mode interpretation. The second derivative is evaluated on one declared Born–Oppenheimer surface, so changing the exchange–correlation approximation, spin branch, cell, or boundary condition changes the force-constant problem rather than merely changing a numerical setting.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Baroni et al., Sec. II.A and Eqs. (5), (10), and (81); Giustino, <cite>Reviews of Modern Physics</cite> 89, 015003 (2017), Sec. III.A, Eqs. (12)–(13).</p>

  <h2>Periodicity organizes displacement patterns by q</h2>
  <p>A crystal contains infinitely many translated copies of the unit cell, so the real-space force-constant matrix is infinite. Translation symmetry solves the organization problem in the same way it organized electronic states in Part III: collective displacement patterns can be classified by a phase between cells. The new label is the phonon or perturbation wavevector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  <p>Do not merge the three reciprocal-space objects. <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> labels an electronic Bloch state; <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> labels a periodic perturbation or lattice normal mode; and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is a reciprocal-lattice vector that identifies equivalent translation characters. Both <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen in one Brillouin zone, but they label different physical objects.</p>
  <p>Fourier transforming the force constants at a chosen <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and dividing by the square roots of the nuclear masses gives the dynamical matrix. With the phase convention used here:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The dynamical matrix is the mass-weighted Fourier transform of real-space force constants, and its eigenvectors are phonon polarizations with eigenvalues equal to squared phonon frequencies">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.8em">
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><munder><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></munder><msub><mi>Φ</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
          <mtr>
            <mtd><munder><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd>
          </mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_&#123;A\alpha,B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;q&#125;)=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\sqrt&#123;M_AM_B&#125;&#125;\sum_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;\Phi_&#123;A\alpha,B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;,\qquad \sum_&#123;B\beta&#125;D_&#123;A\alpha,B\beta&#125;(\mathbf&#123;q&#125;)e_&#123;B\beta,\nu&#125;(\mathbf&#123;q&#125;)=\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;^2e_&#123;A\alpha,\nu&#125;(\mathbf&#123;q&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The first line compresses all cell-to-cell force responses with phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e to the i q dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. The mass factors matter because equal energy curvature does not give equal acceleration to different nuclei. The second line is an eigenvalue problem: branch index <math class="math-inline" aria-label="nu"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> distinguishes different collective patterns at the same <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>; eigenvector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e q nu"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> gives the polarization in mass-weighted coordinates, so the physical displacement of atom <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is proportional to <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e A alpha nu divided by square root M A"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><msqrt><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_&#123;A\alpha,\nu&#125;/\sqrt&#123;M_A&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> times the mode amplitude; the eigenvalue is the squared angular frequency <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega q nu squared"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;^2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  <p>A different Fourier sign convention changes phases in the intermediate definitions, not the physical frequencies. This is one reason a raw eigenvector phase is not itself an observable.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Baroni et al., Sec. II.D.1, Eqs. (80)–(84); Giustino, Sec. III.A, Eqs. (14)–(18).</p>

  <h2>The one-dimensional chain makes a phonon visible</h2>
  <p>Return to the one-dimensional lattice from Part III, now allowing its sites to move. Identical masses <math class="math-inline" aria-label="M"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> sit a distance <math class="math-inline" aria-label="a"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> apart and neighboring sites are joined by harmonic springs of constant <math class="math-inline" aria-label="K"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. A collective displacement in which cell <math class="math-inline" aria-label="m"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> has phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q m a"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qma</annotation></semantics></math> solves the harmonic equations with:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="A plane-wave displacement of a monatomic harmonic chain has angular frequency two times the square root of spring constant over mass times the absolute sine of q a over two">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.75em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>u</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi>ω</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mi>M</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">sin</mi><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_m(t)=u\,\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;(qma-\omega t)&#125;,\qquad \omega(q)=2\sqrt&#123;\frac&#123;K&#125;&#123;M&#125;&#125;\left|\sin\!\left(\frac&#123;qa&#125;&#123;2&#125;\right)\right|.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The first line is not a trajectory assigned independently to every atom. It is one collective pattern: neighboring cells share the same amplitude but differ by phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q a"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qa</annotation></semantics></math>. The physical displacement is the real part of the compact complex expression; the exponential keeps relative phases easy to combine. Substitution into the nearest-neighbor spring equations produces the second line. The spring stiffness raises the frequency, the mass lowers it, and the sine records interference between the forces from the two neighbors.</p>
  <p>The limiting case carries the main physical lesson. As <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q approaches zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, neighboring phases become equal and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega approaches zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The whole chain is translated without changing any spring length, so no restoring force appears. This acoustic zero mode is a consequence of translation invariance, not a failed calculation. If the unit cell contains more than one atom, additional branches can have atoms moving against one another; these are the optical branches. Their name describes the mode family, not a guarantee that every such mode is optically active.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Giustino, Sec. III.A; Martin, 2nd ed., Secs. 20.1–20.4. The chain is an Atlas teaching model, not a claim that real force constants are nearest-neighbor or one-dimensional.</p>

