  <h2 id="dynamical-stability">A phonon is a normal-mode quantum, not arbitrary atomic motion</h2>
  <p>The dynamical-matrix eigenvector defines one independent harmonic normal coordinate. Classically, that coordinate oscillates at <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega q nu"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Quantizing that harmonic oscillator gives energy quanta called phonons. A thermal snapshot or molecular-dynamics trajectory is generally a superposition of many modes and is not itself “one phonon.”</p>
  <p>If the dynamical matrix has a negative eigenvalue, then <math class="math-inline" aria-label="omega squared is negative"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^2&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Plotting codes often represent this by a negative or imaginary frequency. The atoms do not oscillate at an imaginary rate. The result means that the harmonic energy falls along that collective displacement direction, so the reference structure is not a local minimum within the stated model and reciprocal-space point. Exact translational zero modes must be distinguished from such a nontrivial soft direction.</p>
  <p>Evidence must cover the reciprocal-space claim. A calculation at <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Gamma"><semantics><mi>Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> tests only cell-periodic distortions. A plotted high-symmetry path samples selected lines, not the full Brillouin zone. Neither can generally exclude an instability at an unsampled <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, every tiny negative value is not automatically physical: incomplete electronic, basis, supercell, or <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-space convergence can perturb the result. The claimed mode and its error sources must be resolved together.</p>
  <p>Long-wavelength polar crystals add a physical boundary: macroscopic electric fields make the dynamical matrix nonanalytic near <math class="math-inline" aria-label="q equals zero"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;=0</annotation></semantics></math>, producing direction-dependent longitudinal–transverse behavior. Core notes that boundary without deriving the dielectric and Born-effective-charge correction.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Sources: Baroni et al., Secs. II.D.1–II.D.3; Giustino, Sec. III.A. The polar long-wavelength boundary is treated by Baroni et al., Sec. II.D.2. Compare the cross-paper evidence in <a href={href('/reading/literature/synthesis/dynamical-stability-in-2d/')}>Dynamical stability in two-dimensional materials</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="electron-phonon-coupling">The displaced lattice changes the electronic Hamiltonian</h2>
  <p>Phonons would be disconnected from electronic structure if a displacement changed only the nuclear kinetic energy. It does more: moving the nuclei changes the electron–nucleus potential, the electron density responds, and therefore the self-consistent Kohn–Sham potential changes. The derivative of that screened potential along a normalized phonon mode is the bridge between lattice response and electronic states.</p>
  <p>Translation symmetry determines which electronic sector this perturbation can connect. Under a lattice translation <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, the initial Bloch state carries phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e to the i k dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, while a <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>-labelled perturbation contributes <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e to the i q dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Their product has translation character <math class="math-inline" aria-label="e to the i k plus q dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;q&#125;)\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. The connected final state must therefore belong to the <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k plus bold q"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> sector, modulo a reciprocal-lattice vector.</p>
  <p>Using the convention of Giustino’s review, let <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Delta q nu v K S"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>KS</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;KS&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> include the mode polarization, mass factors, and zero-point displacement amplitude. This notation describes a multiplicative Kohn–Sham potential; nonlocal core treatments or generalized-Kohn–Sham constructions replace it by the corresponding first-order operator. The first-order electron–phonon matrix element is:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="The electron phonon matrix element is the unit-cell matrix element of the normalized phonon-induced change in the Kohn Sham potential between an initial electronic Bloch state n k and a final state m k plus q">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>KS</mtext></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mtext>cell</mtext></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_&#123;mn\nu&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;,\mathbf&#123;q&#125;)=\left\langle u_&#123;m,\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;q&#125;&#125;\middle|\Delta_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;KS&#125;&#125;\middle|u_&#123;n,\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;\right\rangle_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cell&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the expression as a transition amplitude. The initial cell-periodic electronic state has band label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and crystal momentum <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. A phonon of branch <math class="math-inline" aria-label="nu"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> and wavevector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> supplies the lattice-periodic factor of the <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>-character potential change. The final electronic state has label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="m comma k plus q"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, understood modulo a reciprocal vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. The unit-cell inner product measures how strongly that particular potential change connects the two states.</p>
  <p>The matrix element <math class="math-inline" aria-label="g"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a coupling object, not by itself a mobility, linewidth, resistivity, mass enhancement, superconducting transition temperature, or proof of superconductivity. Each downstream observable combines many matrix elements with occupations, energy conservation, phase space, screening, and additional approximations. A large value for one mode or a large aggregate coupling parameter does not establish any one of those claims without the corresponding theory and evidence. Continue with <a href={href('/reading/literature/synthesis/from-dfpt-to-tc/')}>From DFPT to a phonon-mediated Tc</a> for the contractions and their evidence boundaries.</p>
  <p>The equation is written with a Kohn–Sham potential because practical first-principles EPC calculations commonly use a DFT/DFPT construction. Its electronic labels remain Bloch labels because the periodic Kohn–Sham Hamiltonian is a periodic one-particle Hamiltonian, as Parts III and IV already established; phonons did not create band theory, and DFT did not create Bloch’s theorem.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Giustino, Sec. III.B.2, especially Eqs. (33)–(38), together with the 2019 erratum, <cite>Reviews of Modern Physics</cite> 91, 019901. The normalization convention for <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Delta q nu v K S"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Δ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>KS</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_&#123;\mathbf&#123;q&#125;\nu&#125;v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;KS&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is stated explicitly because other texts distribute the zero-point and mass factors differently.</p>

  <figure class="reciprocal-roles" aria-labelledby="reciprocal-roles-caption">
    <ol class="atlas-panel-grid atlas-panel-grid--3">
      <li><strong>Electronic state</strong><span><math class="math-inline" aria-label="n bold k"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></span><small>initial Bloch state</small></li>
      <li><strong>Lattice perturbation</strong><span><math class="math-inline" aria-label="nu bold q"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu,\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></span><small>phonon branch and wavevector</small></li>
      <li><strong>Connected state</strong><span><math class="math-inline" aria-label="m bold k plus bold q"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></span><small>equivalent labels may differ by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math></small></li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption id="reciprocal-roles-caption">Electron–phonon coupling brings <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold q"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;q&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> into one expression without making them the same kind of vector.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="concept-check" role="note" aria-labelledby="part-vii-check">
    <h3 id="part-vii-check">Check your understanding</h3>
    <p><strong>Question.</strong> Why can finite displacement and DFPT produce the same phonon frequencies even though one uses displaced supercells and the other solves a linear-response problem?</p>
    <p><strong>Answer.</strong> Both can evaluate the same second derivatives of the same Born–Oppenheimer energy surface. Finite displacement estimates them from differences between nearby self-consistent solutions; DFPT differentiates the self-consistent equations at the reference state. They agree only to the extent that the physical model, structure, boundaries, harmonic regime, and their distinct numerical errors have been aligned and controlled.</p>
  </div>

