  <p>We now know how derivatives of a ground-state energy and potential produce response functions, phonon modes, and electron–phonon coupling objects. But not every spectrum is a ground-state derivative. Adding or removing an electron and creating a neutral optical excitation ask about different many-body states. The next question is therefore: what changes when the target is an excitation rather than a ground-state property?</p>

